# Ask before touching the device

The Blackhole/Wormhole card is a shared resource. Multiple users on the box run
long-lived servers (e.g. `warm_render_server`) and interactive debugger sessions
(`pytest --pdb`) that hold `CHIP_IN_USE_*_PCIe`, so blindly launching a device
command can deadlock, kill someone else's session, or be killed itself.

## Rule

Do NOT run any command that opens the device. Instead, tell the user exactly
what command you would run and wait for them to run it (or explicitly approve).

## What counts as a device command

- `pytest ...` for any test that constructs a ttnn device / mesh (nearly all
  `tests/ttnn/**`, `models/**/tests/**`, `tests/tt_metal/**` end-to-end tests)
- Any Python script that calls `ttnn.open_device`, `ttnn.CreateDevice`,
  `open_mesh_device`, `ttnn.experimental.*` op invocations, etc.
- LLK tests that program silicon (`tt_metal/tt-llk/tests/**` runners)
- Standalone binaries under `build_*/test/**` that touch a chip
- Profiler / tracy captures that need a live device

## What is fine to run without asking

- `./build_metal.sh` and other pure host builds (no device open)
- File reads, grep, glob, `git ...`
- `python -c "import ttnn; print(ttnn.Tile((1,32)))"` and similar host-only
  imports that do NOT call `open_device`
- `ps`, `ls`, `head`, `cat` of terminal / log files

## How to ask

Give the user a copy-pasteable command and the reason, e.g.:

> Ready to verify the fix. Please run:
> ```
> pytest 'tests/.../test_fused_experts.py::test_...[block0-...-batch1-...]' -x --tb=short
> ```
> Paste the failure summary (or "passed") back and I'll continue.

Do not schedule, background, or "just try" a device command yourself.
